          "reasoning_content": "The alert shows log_loss = 25.23 vs threshold 0.54. The predictions show probability = 1.0 for all 5 sampled rows, model_version = sha256:cbf03abb... which matches both reference_model and active_model in the catalog.\n\nAccuracy 0.288, recall 0.977, precision 0.269, confusion matrix [[14, 353], [3, 130]]. So the model predicts churn=1 for almost everyone (353+130=483 predicted positive out of 500), predicting probability 1.0. That's a degenerate model behavior — predicting probability 1.0 for everyone causes massive log loss when actuals include negatives.\n\nConfusion matrix [[TN=14, FP=353], [FN=3, TP=130]] — wait, typically confusion matrix is [[TN, FP], [FN, TP]]. So TN=14, FP=353, FN=3, TP=130. Total actual negatives = 14+353=367, actual positives = 3+130=133. Predictions: positive for 483, negative for 17.\n\nLog loss with probability 1.0 for actual negatives → -log(1-1.0) = -log(0) = infinity. But observed is 25.23, finite, so probabilities aren't exactly 1.0 everywhere (maybe close to 1 like 0.999...). Actually with probability = 1.0 exactly, log loss would be inf. So maybe probabilities are like 0.9999 or so, clipped.\n\nThis looks like a deployment regression — the model started predicting probability ~1.0 for everyone. But the model version is the same as reference_model. Hmm. Both models have the same version sha256:cbf03abb... So the model version didn't change.\n\nWait — the confusion matrix shows almost all predictions are positive. Recall 0.977 means almost all actual positives caught. Precision 0.269 means lots of false positives. This pattern suggests the model outputs near-1 probabilities for everyone — a collapsed model output.\n\nPossible causes:\n- deployment_regression: model serving outputs degenerated (e.g., all probabilities = 1.0)\n- data drift: input distribution changed such that model outputs all high probabilities\n- concept drift: relationship changed\n\nThe model version is identical between reference and active, so a model version change isn't the cause. But deployment_regression could still be the cause (e.g., preprocessing bug in serving pipeline producing degenerate inputs).\n\nLet me check predictions distribution — are all probabilities = 1.0? Let me check data quality on predictions and inputs, and compare source vs inputs to see if data was transformed differently. Also compare_distributions between source and reference_clients to check data drift.\n\nLet me first check describe_resource on predictions to see probability distribution stats, and check source vs inputs comparison. Also compare_model_versions — but both models have same version, so that comparison would likely show no difference. Actually compare_model_versions runs both models on same inputs; if versions are identical, no difference expected. That wouldn't explain the issue.\n\nThe key question: did the model itself change? Same version. Is it data? Let me compare source vs inputs (same 500 customers presumably) to check transformations. And compare_distributions source vs reference_clients to check drift.\n\nLet me do:\n1. describe_resource predictions (probability distribution)\n2. compare_tables source vs inputs (key customer_id)\n3. compare_distributions source vs reference_clients\n\nActually, let me be efficient. The most diagnostic: check probability distribution in predictions (describe_resource), compare source vs inputs (same customers), compare_distributions source vs reference_clients.\n\nLet me start with describe_resource on predictions and compare_tables source vs inputs in parallel.",
